## From Copilot when asked about a dedicated testing spec file:

Create specs/instructions/testing.instructions.md — but scope it tightly to what an AI agent or new contributor needs to produce correct, consistent tests without guessing. Specifically:

Do not duplicate what's already in the CI pipeline spec (tool execution order, pipeline gating) or in AGENTS.md (philosophical mandates). Instead, reference those files and focus the new spec on how to write and organize tests, not when to run them.

When you create it, also clean up the scattered testing bullets in backend.instructions.md, frontend.instructions.md, and AGENTS.md to point to the new spec as the canonical source, keeping those files focused on their own domain concerns. This prevents the current drift from getting worse.

Unified Testing Standards & Strategy
1) Test Tooling by Layer
Backend (Python):
pytest (define version)
Plugins:
pytest-cov (coverage)
pytest-asyncio (async support)
Frontend (Vue/JS):
vitest (define version)
Infrastructure (CDK):
CDK assertions / snapshot tests
E2E (if used):
Define standard (Playwright or Cypress)
2) Directory Structure & Naming Conventions
Define per layer:
Backend: tests/, naming test_*.py
Frontend: __tests__/ or co-located, naming *.test.ts
Standardize across repo to avoid fragmentation
3) Test Categories & Tagging

Define and enforce categories:

Unit – isolated logic, no external dependencies
Integration – service-to-service or API-level tests
Contract – API/schema validation between components
Smoke – basic health/critical path checks
E2E – full user workflows

Include:

Tagging/markers (e.g., @pytest.mark.integration, Vitest equivalents)
CI selection rules (which tests run when)
4) Mocking Strategy

Standardize per layer:

Backend:
Mock AWS at boundary
Preferred tools:
moto (lightweight AWS mocking)
or LocalStack (for higher fidelity, if needed)
Frontend:
API mocking:
MSW (preferred for realism)
or vi.mock for unit-level mocks
Define when to use:
mocks vs real integrations
5) Test Data Strategy
Use:
Fixtures (pytest / vitest)
Factories for dynamic data
Define:
Seed data lifecycle (setup/teardown)
Isolation between tests
Avoid reliance on shared/static state
6) Coverage Policy
Define:
Minimum coverage threshold (e.g., 80–90%)
Enforce:
CI failure if below threshold
Report:
Coverage output (e.g., HTML, CI summary)
7) Integration Test Execution Contract

Define explicitly:

Required:
Environment variables
Target environment (e.g., UAT)
Pass criteria:
What constitutes success/failure
Stability controls:
Retry policy
Timeout thresholds
Handling of flaky tests
8) QA Environments & Test Isolation
Define:
Environment tiers (dev / UAT / prod)
Ensure:
Test database seeding per run
Cleanup/teardown after tests
No cross-test/environment contamination
9) Accessibility & Visual Testing (Frontend)
Define required tooling:
axe-core or pa11y for a11y testing
Clarify expectations:
Automated checks vs manual review
Optional:
Visual regression tools
10) End-to-End (E2E) Testing Strategy
Decide and standardize:
Playwright (recommended) or Cypress
Define:
Scope (critical user flows only)
When E2E tests run in CI
Environment used (usually UAT)
Key Gaps This Resolves

This unified list ensures:

Test runners are explicitly defined (previously missing)
Clear test taxonomy and execution model
Consistent mocking and data strategy
Defined CI enforcement (coverage, retries, environments)
Coverage of frontend a11y and E2E, which were previously undefined